The system said that my previous note was deleted, so just in case here is an update to it.
-------
Thanks for your notes Baptiste and Yihui.  I think that the syntax of pyexpander is cleaner for this purpose, and that Yihui's solution applies more to manipulating code chunks that it applies to mixtures of LaTeX sections and code chunks.  Pyexpander also allows full use of python code, and its string manipulation functions allow you to easily manipulate variable names, e.g. capitalizing the first word or appending a number to the end of the name.  The the R code that is emitted and that pretty prints with knitr has variable names nicely substituted.  The downside is that you have to escape R and LaTeX $ with \$.  Everything I've learned about this, with an example, is at

http://biostat.mc.vanderbilt.edu/KnitrHowto#Using_a_Macro_Preprocessor_with

The one problem I'm having is that I've created a .pye extension for files containing the macro code, and editing these in Emacs with ESS does not give me R NOWEB mode any more.

Frank

