From: ucc1ef8c8158d6b73 at groups.google.com (Yihui Xie)
Date: Wed, 31 Oct 2012 01:12:56 +0000
Subject: Re: [knitr] support for legacy markdown formats?
Message-ID: <MwCQm3pdki4.cBOodUUWZ4MJ@knitr.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <MwCQm3pdki4.2e0SNnkaB8MJ@knitr.groups.google.com>
References: <MwCQm3pdki4.2e0SNnkaB8MJ@knitr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/knitr/c/MwCQm3pdki4/m/cBOodUUWZ4MJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The input syntax is orthogonal to the output, e.g. you can use the
HTML syntax for Markdown, but render Markdown output as well. To
override the default syntax, you can use pat_html().

library(knitr)
pat_html()
render_markdown()

knit(text = '
<!--begin.rcode chunckname
1+1
end.rcode-->
')

I guess it should be easy to convert the legacy markdown syntax to the
current syntax, e.g. (not tested)

for (f in list.files(pattern = '_knit_\\.md$')) {
  txt = readLines(f)
  txt = gsub('^<!--begin.rcode(.*)', '```{r \\1}', txt)
  txt = gsub('\\s*end.rcode-->', '```', txt)
  writeLines(txt, f)
}

Regards,
Yihui
